      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Aspirin and statins are both effective for primary prevention of coronary heart disease (CHD), but their combined use has not been well studied."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To perform a cost-utility analysis of the effects of aspirin therapy, statin therapy, combination therapy with both drugs, and no pharmacotherapy for the primary prevention of CHD events in men."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Markov model."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
          "content": "Published literature."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TARGET POPULATION",
          "content": "Middle-aged men without a history of cardiovascular disease at 6 levels of 10-year risk for CHD (2.5%, 5%, 7.5%, 10%, 15%, and 25%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TIME HORIZON",
          "content": "Lifetime."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "PERSPECTIVE",
          "content": "Third-party payer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Low-dose aspirin, a statin, both drugs as combination therapy, or no therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURE",
          "content": "Cost per quality-adjusted life-year gained."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS OF BASE-CASE ANALYSIS",
          "content": "For 45-year-old men who do not smoke, are not hypertensive, and have a 10-year risk for CHD of 7.5%, aspirin was more effective and less costly than no treatment. The addition of a statin to aspirin therapy produced an incremental cost-utility ratio of 56,200 dollars per quality-adjusted life-year gained compared with aspirin alone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS OF SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Excess risk for hemorrhagic stroke and gastrointestinal bleeding with aspirin, risk for CHD, the cost of statins, and the disutility of taking medication had important effects on the cost-utility ratios."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LIMITATIONS",
          "content": "Several input parameters, particularly adverse event rates and utility values, are supported by limited empirical data. Results are applicable to middle-aged men only."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Compared with no treatment, aspirin is less costly and more effective for preventing CHD events in middle-aged men whose 10-year risk for CHD is 7.5% or higher. The addition of a statin to aspirin therapy becomes more cost-effective when the patient's 10-year CHD risk before treatment is higher than 10%."
        }
      ]},
